From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 18:16:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 91/91] web-app: LanguageTool spelling & grammar check in the
 composer

The desktop targets ship with Chromium spellcheck off (Electron has no
local hunspell dictionary, see target-electron windows/main.ts), and a
browser grammar add-on can't reach into an Electron/Tauri window. So the
composer gets its own LanguageTool integration, like the LibreOffice
extension or the Obsidian community plugin, built on plain fetch so it
works the same on browser, Electron and Tauri.

Settings -> Experimental -> Composer: a "LanguageTool spelling & grammar"
switch (off by default -- the draft leaves the device, outside the E2E
encryption) and a "LanguageTool server" row, shown while off too so a
self-hosted server can be set up before anything is sent. Its dialog picks
languagetool.org (free; or Premium with username + API key, which switches
to api.languagetoolplus.com) or an own server URL (http://host:8081, /v2
and /v2/check suffixes accepted), with a "Check connection" button that
runs a test sentence and shows the error otherwise (CORS / --allow-origin
being the usual one).

Composer: 1s after typing stops the draft is POSTed form-encoded to
/v2/check with language=auto (a CORS simple request, so a self-hosted
server needs no OPTIONS handling); the previous request is aborted on the
next edit. Issues are underlined (red: misspelling, blue: style, amber:
everything else, as in LanguageTool's own UI) in a transparent, click-through copy of the text laid over the
textarea -- a textarea can't style parts of its own text and the CSS
Highlight API can't reach inside one. The copy mirrors the textarea's box
and font metrics (re-measured by a ResizeObserver) and its scrollTop.
Between checks, results are remapped onto the edited text by a common
prefix/suffix diff: matches before the edit keep their offset, matches
after it shift, and a match is dropped once the edit reaches it or the
character next to it (remapMatches, covered by languageTool.test.ts).
Clicking/tapping or arrowing into an underline opens a card anchored to
that word: above it (below when that would leave the window), its pointer
on the word, an accent bar in the issue's colour. It shows LanguageTool's
category name (in the text's language, e.g. "Possible Typo"), the message,
up to five replacements as chips (the first, likely one filled), a quiet
Ignore (per session) and a close button. Card and overlay share a
containing block, so the overlay span's offsets place the card directly.
Typing closes it, so it never pops up mid-sentence. It doesn't own any keys, so Enter still sends; Tab
reaches its buttons. A replacement goes through setRangeText() plus an
`input` event, so the field's own onChange takes it like typing and the
caret lands after it -- no per-field plumbing. The overlay reserves 4px
below the box (height and bottom padding alike, so the scroll range is
unchanged) for the last line's wavy underline, which overshoots a tight
line-height: normal line box.

While LanguageTool is on, the field opts out of the browser's spellcheck
(spellcheck=false) and of the LanguageTool browser add-on
(data-lt-active=false), so there is one set of squiggles. While it is off
nothing changes: the composer keeps spellcheck=true, the other fields keep
the browser default, and an installed add-on works as before.

The same check runs in the profile bio (EditProfileDialog), the group
description (EditGroupNameDialog) and the new-group description
(CreateChat): DeltaTextarea takes an opt-in `languageTool` prop rather
than checking every DeltaTextarea, because the proxy dialog uses one for
proxy URLs, which must not be sent anywhere.

CSP: Electron's main.html and Tauri's connect-src allow
http(s)://*:*/v2/check -- any host/port, pinned to that one path, the
same pattern the web edition uses for /unfurl.

SettingsSelector takes forkAdded like SettingsSwitch, so the server row
gets the sloth badge too; the badge markup (until now copied into
SettingsSwitch and EmojiSetSelector) is one exported ForkBadge.

Styles live in their own composer/_language-tool.scss, so the upstream
_composer.scss is untouched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
